00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.230 align:start position:0%
 
russian opposition leader alexi navalny  remains hospitalized here in berlin in a  medically induced coma  he's recovering from what german doctors  say was most likely  poisoning but the kremlin has rejected  this diagnosis  saying german doctors were too hasty  with their conclusions  now this today from a spokesman kremlin  spokesman  we are categorically against someone  labeling this current situation of  poisoning which  is something that is not confirmed yet  this is a russian citizen who is in a  coma and we would like to find  out the reason for this coma  russia is under increasing pressure to  investigate navalny's illness  tests have confirmed have all the  hallmarks of a  nerve agent attack germany is demanding  a full and transparent investigation a  call that's backed by the european union  the united states and the uk  can conserve the reaction of the german  government  is based on the clinical findings of the  treating physicians  and of course these findings are made  without any political influence  the german government expects the  russian authorities to provide  transparent and speedy clarification  and has full confidence in the work of  the medical team treating mr navalny  at charite hospital  all right for more on this i'm joined  now by andreas nick he's a member of the  german parliament's foreign affairs  committee he's also a member of  german chancellor angela merkel's  conservative cdu party  mr nick is in montebaur in western  germany tonight  mr nick it's good to see you again  germany  germany was first to call on russia to  investigate  the european union the u.s and the uk  are now doing the same that's a lot of  pressure in unison is it enough to make  the kremlin do something  i think that remains to be seen the jury  is out uh  we have been very forthcoming in  offering  uh medical treatment to mr navalny first  of all we  hope for his complete and full recovery  and it is a matter of urgency a matter  of great concern  uh it is not a one-off it's  another one in a series of dubious  killings and assassination attempts of  russian opposition  politicians over the last couple of  years  and i think it is there to send a clear  message that being a  leading opposition politician in russia  is a very dangerous role  well what leverage does germany have  with  putin's russia at this point do you have  sent russian diplomats home in protest  before  but even that seemed symbolic and  nothing more do you do you have real  leverage  i think may i remind you that the  european court of  human rights was issuing an interim  measure end of last week which was  instrumental also in getting mr navalny  out of  russia and into the charity hospital i  think what we can do at the moment is  clearly call upon a full  and transparent investigation this has  to be conducted prompt  independent and effective we are  clearly looking at that where as a  matter of urgency it is uh  as i think as also our foreign ministry  has made clear  this will not really remain unanswered  and we keep all our options and  instruments open as we speak you know mr  nick  before alexi navalny was medevaced out  of siberia and brought here to berlin  the story was mainly about a german  ngo trying to help a russian political  dissident  as soon as mr navalny landed here though  that story  quickly became political is that what  you and your fellow lawmakers in  parliament did you want  it to be that way  i think our government has taken i think  the necessary  measures that someone whose family is  asking for  medical treatment in our country in  berlin and the charity  gets access to that and clearly it is  over symbolic also for symbolic but very  also very practical nature  that uh the personal security of  mr silvani in the charity is secured by  federal agents  of the federal police department  i think we have no interest to  politicize this in the first place i  think this is about human rights this is  about the rule of law  i think it would be a mistake if we were  giving  additional uh  feeding the the the legend that this is  part of a geopolitical  struggle or anything else i think this  is about very fundamental uh  rule of law issues that russia also as a  member state of the council of europe  has to comply with  were you surprised mr nick to see how  quickly the united states also came on  board and demanded  that russia investigate  well i think we have more question marks  about uh  at least the the role of some  representatives of the united states  when it comes to  specific issues regarding russia i think  this may also be not a coincidence that  this is happening  now in the run-up to the us election and  in  in the context of the belarus protests  uh we will we will see i think again  uh all of us i think would be well  advised  not to frame this or to support framing  this into part of a geopolitical  struggle  but it's a very fundamental issue about  the rule of law and  fundamental human rights in the russian  federation  i know i mean i just want to appreciate  this while we have time  germany and the u.s are united in this  case  but when it comes to the nord stream 2  pipeline for example you're not the  trump administration accuses germany of  making itself dependent on russia  for energy do you sometimes feel mr nick  that  germany is caught between a a russian  rock  and an american hard place  i think we are we don't uh think that uh  unilateral sanctions as  applied and threatened by the us  government increasingly are  at all helpful i think they are  counterproductive uh  we strongly advise from refraining from  this kind of action against an  ally like germany and respecting  the political economic sovereignty also  of an allied nation  i think is is one of the core elements  of our alliance  nonetheless i think we have uh issues  with regard  to the russian federation where i think  we are hopefully in broad agreement that  uh  that we we ask uh strongly asked russia  to  come back to the peace and security  order  that has been that has been agreed in  the carter of paris in 1990  which i think for us is still the key  point of reference of living together in  europe  andreas nick member of the german  parliament's foreign affairs committee  as always mr nick we appreciate your  time and  your insights tonight thank you thank  you  well the cinema for peace foundation has  played a key role in bringing  russian opposition activists here to  berlin for treatment at the  charity hospital it did so with a [ __ ]  riot activist  in 2018 and it medevaced alexis navalny  from siberia  to berlin just last weekend now the  group's chair  jaka biezel told dw's chief political  editor mikela coffner  how the arrangements were made  jakar bizil you are the head of cinema  for peace but most of all at the moment  you are the man who brought  alexay navalny to germany how safe do  you feel right now well  there have been a few threats but i've  experienced this before so i don't  really feel unsafe  i think that what happens when you deal  with opposition leaders from countries  which are not a full democracy  who did you call first because it's not  easy to just get a permit to enter  russia just like that  well first i called the charity and  professor aina had been professor  eckhart  to find out if they would be willing to  help again like they did two years ago  and was kind of a bit funny funny  because the office said like oh you're  calling finally we've been expecting the  car ready since this morning since we've  seen the news  so they they thought that it could be a  similar case possibly  how much assistance did you get from  that first moment from the german  government  um actually we informed them about  things that we were doing  but the very first moment was more the  logistical work obviously we were aware  of the fact that angela merkel and  macron had been  giving some statements and we're  offering medical help in their countries  so we felt like that there should not be  any obstacles from that side  how has the last week changed your view  of the political reality  in europe well i mean what we are proud  of in the west is that we have so-called  western values and democracies  and i think after what has been  happening with donald trump  what has been happening with corona it  felt like that there's an erosion  of democratic values  and also of european unity i think if  the west wants to stay the west  it needs to take a clear stand for human  rights and democracy  and it also needs to prove that in  relationship to the rest of the world  what's your biggest hope and your  biggest concern right now  well my hope would be that the patient  will come back to normal fully  i saw last night by chance's wife julia  again  she seemed kind of like  [Music]  okay i have the feeling that  navalny could be doing a little bit  better now by now  so my hope would be that actually the  whole thing will be resolved in a sense  that  he will be able to do his work again and  then maybe the whole case can also  remind  our countries to work on russia  to to protect as journalists and  opposition politicians  and not to be a country where people  like annapolis kaya boris nemtsov  or nova navalny have to fear for their  life  jaka bazil thank you very much for  talking to us  thank you

